   "note": "Estimate, not a survey. The Richmond-Petersburg Turnpike (1955-58) runs through the ward in a trench up to about 6 m deep along its old cross streets. Inside this outline the lidar is set aside and the ground rebuilt as the smoothest surface (a membrane) that meets the measured ground at the outline and carries each street listed here through at grade, straight between its surviving ends. The outline is today's motorway and ramp ways (OpenStreetMap) buffered 22 m (10 m for ramps), widened over the cut slopes the lidar shows (slopes over 12% within 60 m of a motorway), and simplified; east of 1st and Duval streets it keeps only the main line (30 m either side). Against the USGS Richmond sheet surveyed 1933-34 (10-foot contours) the rebuilt ground lies in the same 10-foot band the sheet shows; the error is put at about 1.5 m on the level and up to 3 m at the outline's ends (ground.md in the story folder).",
   "sources": [
    "https://apps.nationalmap.gov/3depdem/",
    "https://prd-tnm.s3.amazonaws.com/StagedProducts/Maps/HistoricalTopo/PDF/VA/24000/VA_Richmond_186500_1934_24000_geo.pdf",
